ss yds/game</strong> (vs ~230).</p><p><strong>CeeDee Lamb</strong> draws a plus matchup \u2014 WAS concede <strong>68 yds/game to WR1s</strong> (league ~58), and DAL funnel 24% of targets his way. A rec-yards spot.</p><p>Sneaky value on <strong>Stefon Diggs</strong> (WR2) \u2014 DAL leak 43 yds/game to the WR2 role, above the ~36 norm.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Jake Ferguson</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 WAS are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.35 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p><strong>CeeDee Lamb</strong> carries TD equity \u2014 WAS surrender 0.51 WR1 receiving scores a game.</p><p><strong>Stefon Diggs</strong> carries TD equity \u2014 DAL surrender 0.59 WR1 receiving scores a game.</p><p>In the red zone DAL throw it (58% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>CeeDee Lamb</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>A modest lean to DAL \u2014 they should lead more often than not, nudging them toward the run late and giving <strong>Javonte Williams</strong> a gentle game-script tailwind.</p><h3>DAL \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>DAL are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (44% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>Javonte Williams</strong> (16 carries, 68 yards projected) gets a mild game-script tailwind as a favorite, though DAL's willingness to keep throwing when ahead caps how much the lead converts into extra carries.</p><p><strong>CeeDee Lamb</strong> projects for <strong>11 targets and 94 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Jake Ferguson</strong> (TE, 4 targets, 29 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>WAS \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>WAS are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>38%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 WAS project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Jacory Croskey-Merritt</strong>'s rushing projection (19 carries, 83 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Stefon Diggs</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, WAS funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>9 targets for 74 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Chig Okonkwo</strong> (TE, 6 targets, 45 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 4-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"WAS","col":"#